<div class="header"><h2>yeast extract and sodium can form MSG during cooking</h2></div>
<div class="body">

<p>The previous section noted that the FDA's <em>"monosodium glutamate"</em> labeling rule applies only when MSG has been <em>added</em> as an ingredient. This section follows that observation one step further: when a vendor adds a free-glutamate source such as <strong>yeast extract</strong> together with sodium-bearing ingredients (table salt, soy sauce, cheese, broth, etc.) and the dish is heated, the same MSG molecule can form <span class="highlight">during cooking</span> &mdash; without the vendor having ever added MSG.</p>

<h3>is hydrolyzed yeast a hidden name for msg?</h3>
<p><em>No.</em> Hydrolyzed yeast is not a hidden name for MSG; it is a declared separate ingredient whose free glutamate combines with sodium already in the recipe to form MSG in the pan. The factory didn't add MSG; the chemistry did. The misleading framing is to call it a "hidden name"; the accurate framing is to call it a <span class="highlight">component that produces the same molecule under heat</span> <sup><a href="#src-fda">fda</a></sup>. That distinction &mdash; hidden name vs. component &mdash; is what this section is about, and the same logic applies to every natural free-glutamate source named below, not just hydrolyzed yeast.</p>

<h3>the FDA names yeast extract as a free-glutamate-plus-sodium carrier</h3>
<p>The FDA's own <em>Questions and Answers on Monosodium glutamate (MSG)</em> page, in its section "How can I know if my food contains MSG?", lists yeast extract among the ingredients in which the relevant glutamate + sodium pair is already present:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>"[F]ree glutamate and sodium occur together naturally in ingredients such as hydrolyzed vegetable protein, autolyzed yeast, hydrolyzed yeast, <strong>yeast extract</strong>, soy extracts, and protein isolate, as well as in tomatoes and cheeses." <sup><a href="#src-fda">fda</a></sup></p>
</blockquote>
<p>The FDA does not require any of these ingredients to be labeled with their free-glutamate load:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>"While FDA requires that these ingredients be listed on the ingredient panel, the agency does not require the label to also specify that they naturally contain glutamate." <sup><a href="#src-fda">fda</a></sup></p>
</blockquote>

<h3>natural sources of free glutamate</h3>
<p>Yeast extract is one example of a broader class. <em>Natural sources of glutamate</em> &mdash; foods that carry meaningful free glutamic acid &mdash; fall into two groups:</p>
<p><strong>Group 1 &mdash; the FDA's own named carriers</strong> (from the quote above): hydrolyzed vegetable protein, autolyzed yeast, hydrolyzed yeast, yeast extract, soy extracts, protein isolate, plus "tomatoes and cheeses" <sup><a href="#src-fda">fda</a></sup>.</p>
<p><strong>Group 2 &mdash; umami-rich natural foods not in the FDA's parenthetical</strong>:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Dried shiitake mushrooms</strong> &mdash; among the highest natural free-glutamate foods (well over 1,000 mg per 100 g dried); glutamate mostly paired with potassium, not sodium <sup><a href="#src-fga-table">fga-table</a></sup>.</li>
<li><strong>Kombu / kelp</strong> &mdash; the original Ikeda source; glutamate paired with potassium <sup><a href="#src-msg-history">msg-history</a></sup>.</li>
<li><strong>Ripe tomatoes</strong> &mdash; ~140 to 246 mg per 100 g in flesh, up to ~456 mg per 100 g in pulp; glutamate paired mostly with potassium and the free acid <sup><a href="#src-fga-table">fga-table</a>, <a href="#src-acs-tomato">acs-tomato</a>, <a href="#src-khymos">khymos</a></sup>.</li>
<li><strong>Aged cheeses</strong>, especially Parmesan (Parmigiano Reggiano, 24-month) &mdash; ~1,200 to 1,680 mg per 100 g; counter-ions are mostly potassium and calcium <sup><a href="#src-fga-table">fga-table</a>, <a href="#src-khymos">khymos</a></sup>.</li>
<li><strong>Soy sauce and miso</strong> &mdash; fermented; carry both free glutamate and a substantial sodium load in the same ingredient.</li>
<li><strong>Fermented fish sauces, Worcestershire sauce</strong> &mdash; long fermentation releases bound glutamate from protein; often carry both free glutamate and sodium.</li>
<li><strong>Broths and stocks</strong> (meat, poultry, fish, bone) &mdash; slow simmering hydrolyzes protein and releases bound glutamate; finished with salt.</li>
<li><strong>Cured and aged meats</strong> (Parma-style ham, anchovies, etc.) &mdash; free glutamate released by curing/aging, plus the sodium from the cure itself.</li>
</ul>
<p>In every one of these foods, the natural cation partner for glutamate is <em>not</em> sodium &mdash; it is potassium, calcium, magnesium, ammonium, or the free acid (H<sup>+</sup>). So by the FDA's strict definition, none of them is "MSG-containing." The full argument for tomatoes and cheese is made in <a href="#section-msg-does-not-occur-in-tomatoes-or-cheese">msg does not occur in tomatoes or cheese</a>; the same non-sodium counterion logic applies to the rest of the list.</p>

<h3>why cooking can convert that pair into MSG</h3>
<p>The same chemistry applies to any of the sources above paired with any sodium-bearing ingredient, not just yeast extract + salt. Free L-glutamic acid (E620) is a weak acid. In a moist, heated food, it is in equilibrium with its salts. When sodium ions are present in the same matrix &mdash; from added salt, soy sauce, brined ingredients, cheese, or naturally occurring sodium in the broth &mdash; a fraction of the free glutamate pairs with sodium to form monosodium glutamate. The chemistry is not exotic: it is the same acid-base equilibrium that converts any glutamic-acid solution + sodium chloride into the glutamate salt. The Löï &amp; Cynober review documents that all five glutamate salts (E620 through E625) dissociate into the same free glutamate anion in aqueous solution, behaving identically to free glutamate in the body <sup><a href="#src-msg-alts-1">msg-alts-1</a></sup>; that reversibility is exactly what lets the glutamate ion <em>pick up</em> whichever cation is available in the cooking matrix.</p>
<p>The extent of the conversion depends on local concentrations, pH, water activity, and temperature &mdash; it is not 100% efficient &mdash; but the qualitative point is the same: a dish built from any of the natural free-glutamate sources above and cooked with sodium-bearing ingredients contains MSG that did not have to be declared as such.</p>

<h3>finished-dish examples</h3>
<p>Six real cases that drive the point home across the full source list:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Tomato sauce + salt.</strong> Ripe tomatoes carry ~140 to 246 mg of free glutamate per 100 g, paired mostly with potassium. A finished sauce with added salt is in the same equilibrium; a fraction of that glutamate pairs with sodium during heating. The label lists "tomatoes" and "salt"; the cooked dish contains in-situ MSG <sup><a href="#src-fga-table">fga-table</a>, <a href="#src-acs-tomato">acs-tomato</a>, <a href="#src-khymos">khymos</a></sup>.</li>
<li><strong>Mushroom risotto with Parmesan and salted butter.</strong> Three natural free-glutamate sources in one dish (mushrooms, aged cheese, and the stock the rice is cooked in), plus sodium from the salt, the butter, and the cheese itself. None declared as MSG; the finished risotto carries MSG formed during cooking <sup><a href="#src-fga-table">fga-table</a>, <a href="#src-khymos">khymos</a></sup>.</li>
<li><strong>Kombu dashi finished with soy sauce.</strong> Kombu is the original Ikeda source &mdash; glutamate paired with potassium, not sodium. The soy sauce adds both more free glutamate and a large sodium load. By the time the dashi is heated and the soy sauce stirred in, a measurable fraction of the glutamate is paired with sodium <sup><a href="#src-msg-history">msg-history</a>, <a href="#src-msg-alts-1">msg-alts-1</a></sup>.</li>
<li><strong>Beef or chicken stock, finished with salt.</strong> Slow simmering hydrolyzes protein and releases bound glutamate. The stock is seasoned with salt at the end; the same equilibrium forms a fraction of MSG that was never declared on any ingredient panel.</li>
<li><strong>Soy-mirin glaze.</strong> Soy sauce already contains both free glutamate and sodium, so a meaningful fraction of its glutamate is present as the monosodium salt from the moment it is bottled. Heating with mirin and reducing the glaze shifts the equilibrium further. This case is partly "MSG-already-present-in-the-ingredient" rather than purely "formed in the pan" &mdash; soy sauce sits on the spectrum between a natural glutamate source and an MSG-bearing finished food <sup><a href="#src-msg-alts-1">msg-alts-1</a></sup>.</li>
<li><strong>Miso soup with salted garnishes.</strong> Miso (fermented soy, glutamate mostly potassium-paired) is dissolved in dashi (more free glutamate) and served with salted wakame or salted tofu. Sodium is contributed by the miso itself, the dashi, and the garnishes. The same equilibrium forms MSG in the bowl.</li>
</ol>
<p>None of the six finished dishes declares "monosodium glutamate" on its label. All of them contain MSG.</p>

<h3>why the label still says nothing about this</h3>
<p>The same point applies to every example above: the label discloses components, not the dish's MSG content. The FDA's labeling rule reads "monosodium glutamate" as an <em>added ingredient</em>. The agency does not regulate MSG that forms in situ during cooking from separately-declared components, because by the rule's own wording, none was added. The practical consequence: a label that lists "yeast extract" and "salt" can correspond to a finished dish whose free-glutamate-plus-sodium load meets or exceeds what the same dish would have if it had been seasoned with crystalline MSG. The vendor has done nothing the FDA forbids &mdash; the FDA simply does not require disclosure of the result. And the same goes for "tomatoes" + "salt," for "parmesan" + "butter" + "salt," for "kombu" + "soy sauce," and so on through the examples above.</p>

<h3>one honest caveat</h3>
<p><em>Once ingested, the glutamate ion from yeast-extract-plus-sodium is the same molecule as the glutamate ion from factory-made MSG.</em> The FDA's own page says so. This section makes a <em>labeling and chemistry</em> point: that "yeast extract" (or "tomatoes," or "parmesan," or "mushrooms," or "kombu," or "soy sauce") on an ingredient panel can correspond to a finished food that contains MSG, formed without MSG ever being declared. It is not a claim that in-situ-formed MSG behaves differently in the body from pre-mixed MSG. What <em>is</em> different is the labeling: a consumer who avoids "monosodium glutamate" on principle will not see this form of MSG named on the package.</p>

